kernels.{cu,ptx,sass,cubin.hex,cfg.dot,bbcfg.dot}: the example's own
translation unit, compiled and disassembled by gen.sh.

Why it is here
--------------
The example compiles ../kernels.cu through NVRTC for whichever device it
finds and writes the results into a bundle. These fixtures let the tests read
that bundle's contents without a device. gen.sh copies ../kernels.cu into this
directory rather than reaching outside it, because go:embed cannot: the
package's testdata has to be self-contained while the unit keeps one author.
A cmp of the two files is the check that the copy is current.

The same generated files used to be a byte-identical copy of
x/cudacapture/testdata's, which were hand-written and named an entry point
"compute" that no __global__ function declared. Sharing them is not possible
for the same go:embed reason, so they are generated here from the source this
example actually compiles.

Toolchain
---------
Generated 2026-09-22 on gpu-host, an NVIDIA GB10 host, with CUDA 13.4 --
nvcc V13.4.59, nvdisasm V13.4.49, NVVM 7.0.1, PTX ISA 9.4. Target sm_90.

No GPU was used: nvcc, ptxas and nvdisasm compile and disassemble, and neither
touches a device. The example itself does launch kernels; these files do not.

The nvdisasm options in gen.sh are the ones capture.go passes, so the fixture
is the disassembly this example really writes. The one difference from a live
run is the compiler: the example goes through NVRTC for the device it finds,
gen.sh goes through nvcc for sm_90.

The control flow graphs are what nvdisasm prints, which is one digraph named
"f" holding a "cluster_<function>" subgraph per function -- not one digraph
per kernel. x/cudasass reads the clusters.

padding.{ptx,sass} and ds4_math.cu are a different fixture with its own
provenance; see padding.README. gen.sh does not touch them.

